synthesis.answer_shape:
An explicit finite formula (finite-state per instance), not an aggregate closed form.

Computing Bd needs only one scan of the coefficients of prod[a_i]_q/[r]_q, up to the first negative one. Then:
- U ⊆ [1,Bd] is proved.
- Conjecturally, U = [1,Bd-2] ∪ (whatever passes the exact tests at b = Bd-1 and b = Bd), so U ∈ {[1,Bd], [1,Bd]\{Bd-1}, [1,Bd-2]}.

The exact proved structure is U = [1,N*] ∪ {N*+2,...,beta}, from about r/2 alternating class-pair sums.

Closed form in aggregates exists only on these domains:
- the flat domain: U = [1,T6], T6 = 1+floor((D+1-2mu)/r);
- the three-middle domain (others ±1): U = [1, T6 - 2·FIRE];
- r | a_i: all b.

Proved non-existence results:
- no aggregate rule (T7, T17);
- no threshold rule (N1);
- no pair-local proof (r=8595);
- "max U = Bd" is false (Thm 6), so the 3-way defect at the top is genuine.
